1	---
2	title: Inheritance & super
3	description: A real three-file hierarchy under the namespace pattern — plain-getter chains through super, same-name computeds that never collide, a receipt built level by level, and import cycles solved by construction.
4	---
5	
6	# Inheritance & `super`
7	
8	Reactive classes inherit like native classes — any depth, across files.
9	Refs, plain getters, computeds, methods and `super` all resolve correctly
10	through the chain, because instances **are** native objects with a
11	transformed prototype chain.
12	
13	The best way to see it is a hierarchy doing real work — a pricing chain.
14	`Product` knows its price. `SaleProduct` applies a discount. `TaxedProduct`
15	adds tax. Each level refines `total` through `super` and appends its own
16	line to the receipt.
17	
18	## Three levels, three files
19	
20	Each file exports the [namespace pattern](/guide/modules): the raw class for
21	extending, the reactive class for instantiating.
22	
23	```ts
24	// Product.ts
25	import { Reactive } from 'ivue';
26	import { ref } from 'vue';
27	
28	class $Product {
29	  get title() {
30	    return ref('Mechanical keyboard');
31	  }
32	  get price() {
33	    return ref(48);
34	  }
35	
36	  get total(): number {
37	    return this.price.value;
38	  }
39	
40	  receipt(): string[] {
41	    return [`${this.title.value} — $${this.price.value.toFixed(2)}`];
42	  }
43	}
44	
45	export namespace Product {
46	  export const $Class = $Product; // raw — children `extends` this
47	  export const Class = Reactive($Class); // reactive — you `new` this
48	  export type Instance = typeof Class.Instance; // defineExpose type & reactive() interop
49	}
50	```
51	
52	```ts
53	// SaleProduct.ts
54	import { Reactive } from 'ivue';
55	import { ref } from 'vue';
56	import { Product } from './Product';
57	
58	class $SaleProduct extends Product.$Class {
59	  get discount() {
60	    return ref(0.2);
61	  }
62	
63	  get total(): number {
64	    return super.total * (1 - this.discount.value);
65	  }
66	
67	  receipt(): string[] {
68	    return [
69	      ...super.receipt(),
70	      `sale −${Math.round(this.discount.value * 100)}%`,
71	    ];
72	  }
73	}
74	
75	export namespace SaleProduct {
76	  export const $Class = $SaleProduct;
77	  export const Class = Reactive($Class);
78	  export type Instance = typeof Class.Instance;
79	}
80	```
81	
82	```ts
83	// TaxedProduct.ts
84	import { Reactive } from 'ivue';
85	import { ref } from 'vue';
86	import { SaleProduct } from './SaleProduct';
87	
88	class $TaxedProduct extends SaleProduct.$Class {
89	  get taxRate() {
90	    return ref(0.1);
91	  }
92	
93	  get total(): number {
94	    return super.total * (1 + this.taxRate.value);
95	  }
96	
97	  receipt(): string[] {
98	    return [
99	      ...super.receipt(),
100	      `tax +${Math.round(this.taxRate.value * 100)}%`,
101	      `due — $${this.total.toFixed(2)}`,
102	    ];
103	  }
104	}
105	
106	export namespace TaxedProduct {
107	  export const $Class = $TaxedProduct;
108	  export const Class = Reactive($Class);
109	  export type Instance = typeof Class.Instance;
110	}
111	```
112	
113	Each level is made of the three shapes every ivue class is made of, and each
114	shape chains through `super` natively: **state** as ref-getters (`price`,
115	`discount`, `taxRate` — each level owns its own), a **derivation** as a plain
116	getter (`total` — each level refines `super.total`), and a **method**
117	(`receipt` — each level extends `super.receipt()`).
118	
119	## One instance, every level live
120	
